The block comment (lines 303–306) claims that non-timestamp formats will throw an error when dayjs strict-parsing fails, but that is not what the code does. `isTimestampFormat` is only used to conditionally emit a duplicate `log.debug` message — the `new Date(str)` fallback executes for *every* format regardless of the flag's value. This makes the comment actively misleading: a future maintainer reading it will believe stricter validation is in place when it is not.

If the intent was to skip the `new Date()` fallback for non-timestamp formats and throw immediately instead, the implementation needs to be completed:
```js
if (!isTimestampFormat) {
  throw new Error(`Invalid date: "${str}" does not match format "${dateFormat.trim()}".`);
}
```
If that was not the intent, both `isTimestampFormat` and the conditional block should be removed entirely.

The `log.warn` message is built by concatenating three separate template literals with `+`. Per project conventions, prefer a single template literal (or no string concatenation at all):
```js
log.warn(
  `The tick interval "${every}${interval}" would generate ${estimatedTicks} ticks, which exceeds the maximum allowed (${MAX_TICK_COUNT}). This may indicate an invalid date or time range. Skipping custom tick interval.`
);
```

`timeScale.domain()` is called independently in both the bottom-axis block (~line 653) and the top-axis block (~line 717). The domain does not change between these two calls. Compute it once before both blocks and reuse the result:
```js
const domain = timeScale.domain();
const minTime = domain[0];
const maxTime = domain[1];
```
This also removes the duplicated `const domain / minTime / maxTime` declarations.
